<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=aquaculture">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aquaculture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 475 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as aquafarming, aquiculture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aquaculture is the farming of fish, crustaceans, molluscs, algae and other aquatic organisms under controlled or semi-natural conditions. Oral tradition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> places the culture of the common carp around 2000–2100 BCE, but the earliest significant evidence is The Classic of Fish Culture, a monograph by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-li">Fan Li</a> written around 475 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> bred fish in ponds and farmed oysters before 100 CE, medieval monasteries took up their methods, and Hawaiians built ocean fishponds. By 1866 artificial fish hatcheries operated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and world output reached 130.9 million tonnes in 2022.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aquaculture.jpg" alt="Aquaculture" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NOAA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abalone-farm1web.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 475 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/fan-li">Fan Li</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Alekoko, Hawaii (at least 1,000 years ago)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Gunditjmara of south-western Victoria, Australia, may have raised eels at Budj Bim as early as about 4,580 BCE, though Peter Sutton disputes that their eel traps were aquaculture. Evidence from Jiahu in China, from 6200 BCE, is speculative.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=aquaculture">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
